          How Dave Ramsey's Retirement Philosophy Works (Baby Step 4)
        </h2>
        <p class="text-body dark:text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Dave Ramsey's personal finance framework revolves around the famous <strong>7 Baby Steps</strong>. Before you invest a single dollar into retirement, Dave requires completing Baby Steps 1, 2, and 3: saving a starter $1,000 emergency fund, eliminating all non-mortgage debt using the debt snowball method, and building a fully funded emergency reserve of 3 to 6 months of expenses.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body dark:text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Once you are debt-free (except the house), you reach <strong>Baby Step 4: Invest 15% of your gross household income into tax-advantaged retirement accounts</strong>. Why exactly 15%? Because contributing less fails to harness the compound interest necessary to build a dignified nest egg, while contributing more limits your cash flow to fund Baby Step 5 (children's college savings) and Baby Step 6 (paying off your home mortgage early).
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-2">Dave's Priority Order of Investing:</h3>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">
            <li><strong>401(k) / 403(b) Match:</strong> Invest in your employer plan up to the exact percentage matched (e.g., if the company matches 4%, contribute 4%). An employer match represents an immediate 100% guaranteed return.</li>
            <li><strong>Roth IRA:</strong> Fund a Roth IRA outside your workplace up to the annual IRS limit ($7,000 for 2024–2026, or $8,000 if age 50+). Roth growth and withdrawals in retirement are 100% tax-free.</li>
            <li><strong>Back to 401(k) / HSA:</strong> If maxing your Roth IRA does not yet equal 15% of your gross annual household income, return to your workplace 401(k) or a Health Savings Account (HSA) to fulfill the remaining dollar amount.</li>
          </ol>
