      <!-- Section 3: The 8% vs 4% Safe Withdrawal Controversy -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">
          The Withdrawal Rate Debate: Dave's 8% vs The 4% Rule
        </h2>
        <p class="text-body dark:text-muted leading-relaxed">
          One of the most fierce debates in modern wealth management centers on Dave Ramsey's claim that retirees can safely withdraw <strong>8% per year</strong> from their nest egg. Dave's mathematical logic is straightforward:
        </p>
        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-primary pl-4 py-2 italic text-sm text-body dark:text-muted bg-canvas-soft dark:bg-surface rounded-r-xl">
          "If the stock market averages 12% per year and inflation averages 4%, you leave 4% in the account to preserve your principal and spend the remaining 8% without ever touching your nest egg."
        </blockquote>
        <p class="text-body dark:text-muted leading-relaxed">
          While this simple arithmetic sounds compelling, certified financial planners (CFPs) point out a critical mathematical flaw known as <strong>Sequence of Returns Risk (SRR)</strong>. The stock market does not deliver a smooth 12% return every year; it experiences severe cyclical downturns (e.g., -37% in 2008 or -22% in 2002).
        </p>
        <p class="text-body dark:text-muted leading-relaxed">
          If a retiree withdraws 8% during a bear market year when their portfolio has declined 20%, the combined depletion will permanently impair the portfolio's compounding capacity. This is why the famous <strong>Trinity Study</strong> established the <strong>4% Safe Withdrawal Rate</strong>, proving that withdrawing 4% (adjusted annually for inflation) survives over 95% of all historical 30-year retirement windows.
        </p>
      </section>

